The session extension persists server-side data associated with an identifier; the cookie normally carries that identifier, not the entire session array. The $_SESSION superglobal array holds the active session data; unset($_SESSION["minutes"]) removes that key. session_start acquires state, and session_write_close saves it and releases the lock. Avoid retaining a session lock while unrelated slow work runs.

This isolated array-only companion shows unset removing the specified key without ending a session or destroying other data. Its input is the selected entry, and the null-coalescing read then uses the missing-key fallback. The session fixture separately verifies saving and reopening that deletion.
```php
<?php
$_SESSION = ["minutes" => 15];
unset($_SESSION["minutes"]);
echo $_SESSION["minutes"] ?? "missing", "\n";
```
Expected output: missing.

Complete local PHP example; the app itself does not execute it:
```php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);
if (!extension_loaded("session")) { throw new RuntimeException("session required"); }
$directory = __DIR__ . "/session-files";
if (!is_dir($directory) && !mkdir($directory, 0700)) { throw new RuntimeException("directory failed"); }
ini_set("session.use_cookies", "0");
ini_set("session.cache_limiter", "");
ini_set("session.use_strict_mode", "1");
session_save_path($directory);
if (!session_start()) { throw new RuntimeException("start failed"); }
$id = session_id();
$_SESSION["minutes"] = 15;
if (!session_write_close()) { throw new RuntimeException("save failed"); }
$_SESSION = [];
session_id($id);
if (!session_start()) { throw new RuntimeException("reload failed"); }
$value = $_SESSION["minutes"] ?? "missing";
if (!session_destroy()) { throw new RuntimeException("destroy failed"); }
echo $value, "\n";

```

